  home_upcard_demo.py (2026-08-29, user request): take-vs-draw
  connectivity ladder, ten game (flip KD), opponent discarded 7H,
  spares all low so taking always worsens raw deadwood (isolates meld
  potential). 384 worlds x 3 seeds; take-minus-draw EV gap:
    0 combos -1.0, pair only -2.6, pair+run (user's 7d/8h example)
    -0.7 TAKE, pair+run+gap +0.0 TAKE, immediate meld +18.5 TAKE.
  Reading: 2 combos is where taking stops COSTING EV (the teacher's
  deadwood tiebreak then tips to take); speculative takes are never
  clearly profitable short of completing a meld. SIDE FINDING from a
  discarded first design: with high junk in hand, taking a mid card
  is worth ~+6 as a pure VALUE SWAP (7 replacing a J/Q) with zero
  combos -- in practice most correct takes are swap-driven, not
  combo-driven.

EVALUATOR ENGINE UPGRADE (2026-08-29, user-caught blind spot)
  On pos_take2_swap (take an unconnected 6C for a 16->13 deadwood
  swap vs draw), the USER out-counted the draw branch: 11/39 unseen
  cards enable an IMMEDIATE next-turn knock (2S/6S/KD at dw 7, three
  aces at 8, 2H/2C at 9, three 3s at 10) while the take enables none
  -- and challenged the evaluator's near-tie. Verified: the v4-family
  engine's rollout hero (patient teacher) never knocks in rollouts,
  so next-turn hero-knock value is priced as mere gin progress. At
  K=1536: flat engine draw +0.74 +/- 0.35; deep 2-ply engine (hero's
  first rollout decision branches over top-2 discards + best knock)
  draw +4.50 +/- 0.39 -- a ~6x undervaluation, exactly the lesson-#12
  asymmetry principle biting in the user-facing tool. FIX:
  evaluate.py now defaults to engine="deep" (AdaptiveExpert single
  stage, deep_from=0; --engine v4 keeps the old one). Re-rendered
  gallery at 768 deep worlds: take1 tie +0.18 (take stands), take2
  DRAW +4.04 +/- 0.57 (flipped, user vindicated), take3 draw (gap
  narrowed to -1.49: the knock-aware engine values the take's
  next-turn knock too), take4 take (ace game, -0.40 tie).
  Also added: text+HTML reports now explicitly flag statistical ties
  where the kept prior is not the top raw mean.
  NOTE (fidelity): the engine's home transform gives the first actor
  one take-the-flip decision; the real table rule also offers the
  DEALER the flip if the non-dealer passes. That second option is not
  modeled (accepted approximation since 2026-08-24).
